.\" Render with: groff -man -Tutf8 smithproxy-capture.7
.TH SMITHPROXY-CAPTURE 7 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
smithproxy-capture \- Smithproxy local and remote traffic capture
.SH DESCRIPTION
Smithproxy can record traffic locally or export it to a remote collector. When
a protocol is terminated by a MITM layer, capture can contain plaintext even
though the traffic is encrypted on both external network legs.
.PP
Capture requires two decisions:
.nf
    content profile:  which connections and how many bytes
    capture target:   where and in which format records are written
.fi
.PP
The matching policy assigns the content profile. The global
.B captures
section configures local files and remote GRE emission.
.SH CAPTURE POINT
For plaintext TCP or UDP, recorded bytes correspond to relayed application
payload. For inspected TLS, SSH or QUIC, the relevant MITM layer can expose
decrypted application data and protocol metadata to capture.
.PP
Pass-through TLS, opaque SSH and unsupported encrypted protocols remain
encrypted. Capture does not imply that Smithproxy understands the application
protocol carried by the bytes.
.SH CONTENT PROFILE
A
.B content_profile
is assigned by policy. Important fields include:
.TP
.B write_payload
Enable payload capture for matching connections.
.TP
.B write_format
Select
.BR smcap ,
.BR pcap
or
.BR pcap_single .
.TP
.B write_limit_client, write_limit_server
Limit recorded bytes in each direction. Zero means the configured unlimited
behavior; confirm limits in the active configuration before production use.
.TP
.B content_rules
Optional payload matching and replacement rules. Replacement changes relayed
traffic and is not merely a capture filter.
.SH LOCAL CAPTURE
The
.B captures.local
group controls local output:
.nf
    captures = {
        local = {
            enabled = TRUE;
            dir = "/var/smithproxy/data";
            file_prefix = "";
            file_suffix = "";
            format = "pcap_single";
            pcap_quota = 100;
        };
    };
.fi
.TP
.B smcap
Per-connection Smithproxy text capture with payload and connection context.
It is useful for inspection and replay tooling but is not a standard packet
capture format.
.TP
.B pcap
One standard PCAP file per captured connection.
.TP
.B pcap_single
Append records from multiple sessions to a shared rotating PCAP. The
.B pcap_quota
value is the rollover size in MiB; zero disables quota-based rollover.
.PP
The default local directory is
.IR /var/smithproxy/data .
Ensure the service user can write it and that rotation/retention matches the
traffic volume.
.SH REMOTE GRE EXPORT
The
.B captures.remote
group exports capture records in GRE:
.nf
    captures = {
        remote = {
            enabled = TRUE;
            tun_type = "gre";
            gre_format = "spq1";
            tun_dst = "192.0.2.50";
            tun_ttl = 1;
        };
    };
.fi
.TP
.B tun_dst
Collector IP address.
.TP
.B tun_ttl
Outer-packet TTL. A small value helps prevent decrypted capture export from
leaving the intended network boundary.
.TP
.B spq1
Smithproxy plaintext packet export. QUIC-related SPQ1 records can use the
supplied Wireshark dissector under
.IR /usr/share/smithproxy/wireshark/ .
.TP
.B pcapng
Native PCAPNG record export. It can carry Enhanced Packet Blocks and Decryption
Secrets Blocks where the protocol path supplies them.
.PP
GRE provides encapsulation, not confidentiality or peer authentication. Protect
the path with network isolation or an appropriate secure tunnel when records
cross an untrusted network.
.SH TLS KEY LOG
Smithproxy can write TLS traffic secrets in SSLKEYLOG-compatible form to the
path configured by
.BR settings.sslkeylog_file .
The matching TLS profile must enable key export.
.PP
SSLKEYLOG material can decrypt compatible packet captures. Treat it as sensitive
as plaintext capture and delete it according to the same retention policy.
.SH QUIC AND HTTP/3
QUIC multiplexes streams inside one UDP connection. Smithproxy's experimental
QUIC listener can export plaintext stream data and decoded HTTP/3 header
metadata. Native PCAPNG export can associate packet records and TLS secrets;
SPQ1 provides Smithproxy-specific plaintext QUIC records for analysis.
.PP
QUIC capture availability depends on OpenSSL QUIC support, listener state and
the negotiated protocol. Generic UDP capture cannot recover QUIC plaintext by
itself.
.SH DIRECTION AND METADATA
Smithproxy tracks client-to-server and server-to-client byte directions on the
logical session. Capture records can also include addresses, ports, timestamps,
connection identifiers and protocol-specific metadata depending on format.
.PP
Because MITM creates two real network connections, synthetic plaintext records
do not necessarily reproduce the packet sizes, sequence numbers, timing or
ciphertext visible on either physical leg.
.SH ROTATION AND STORAGE
Estimate storage from application payload volume, not encrypted wire size alone.
Content limits, global enable switches, PCAP quota and external retention should
be configured together. Monitor free space and capture errors; disk failure
must not be allowed to surprise an inspection host.
.PP
The CLI command
.nf
    execute pcap rollover
.fi
forces rollover of the shared PCAP file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Capture selected HTTPS sessions locally
.nf
    captures = {
        local = {
            enabled = TRUE;
            dir = "/var/smithproxy/data";
            format = "pcap_single";
            pcap_quota = 250;
        };
        remote = { enabled = FALSE; };
    };

    content_profiles = {
        audit = {
            write_payload = TRUE;
            write_format = "pcap_single";
            write_limit_client = 1048576;
            write_limit_server = 4194304;
        };
    };
.fi
.PP
Assign
.B content_profile = "audit"
only to the intended HTTPS policy. The example caps capture per direction while
the shared file rotates at approximately 250 MiB.
.SS Export native records to a collector
.nf
    captures = {
        local = { enabled = FALSE; };
        remote = {
            enabled = TRUE;
            tun_type = "gre";
            gre_format = "pcapng";
            tun_dst = "192.0.2.50";
            tun_ttl = 1;
        };
    };
.fi
.PP
Permit GRE between the proxy and collector and capture the tunnel endpoint
there. This example provides no encryption; keep the path inside a controlled
network or protect it separately.
.SS Export TLS secrets without broad payload capture
Enable SSLKEYLOG in a narrow TLS profile and assign it only to a diagnostic
policy. Capture encrypted packets on the wire and load the resulting key log in
Wireshark. Disable the diagnostic policy and remove the secrets after use.
.SH ANALYSIS
Standard PCAP and PCAPNG files can be opened in Wireshark or tshark. SMCap is
consumed by Smithproxy-oriented tooling such as pplay. Remote GRE export can be
captured on the collector and decoded according to its selected record format.
.PP
When troubleshooting, compare:
.IP \(bu 2
the original wire capture before Smithproxy;
.IP \(bu 2
the plaintext Smithproxy capture;
.IP \(bu 2
the upstream wire capture after Smithproxy.
.PP
These represent different connections and answer different questions.
.SH TESTING POLICY
Since October 2026, successful clean-tree capture validation can be retained as
a commit-specific report under
.IR docs/test-reports/ .
The documented result should identify the format and verify both expected
plaintext visibility and the absence of payload from excluded policies.
.SH SECURITY AND PRIVACY
Captures can contain credentials, cookies, personal data, private keys in
application payloads and complete user activity. Limit capture policies to the
minimum required traffic and byte range. Restrict filesystem permissions,
collector access, backups and retention.
.PP
Do not assume that omitting payload makes all metadata harmless. DNS names,
addresses, SNI, HTTP headers, timing and session identifiers can remain
sensitive.
.SH FAILURE BEHAVIOR
Capture/export failure and traffic forwarding are separate concerns. Verify the
desired fail-open or fail-closed behavior for the deployed version and monitor
logs rather than assuming successful forwarding means successful capture.
